  <circuit name="notes">
    <comp lib="0" loc="(19,40)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#8bf0d0"/>
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">Some notes on these audio circuits....

We use 16kHz signed 16-bit PCM audio output, but try to avoid sounds that are very loud. 
That means the audio system expects to receive:
 * 16000 samples (values) per second.
 * Each sample  (value) should be a signed 16-bit number. 
 * The "neutral" middle sample value is 0.
 * We aim to keep samples in the range [-10000, +10000] so they aren't too loud.
 * The max range for samples is [-32768, +32767]. But that's very loud.

When doing math or arithemetic, we sometimes use 16-bit signed numbers, but we
must keep in mind that any intermediate results need to stay within the [-32768, +32767]
range, otherwise we get distortion or unexpected results. The end result should ideally
be within [-10000, +10000], so it isn't too loud.

More often, when doing math and arithmetic we "widen" everything to 32-bit signed
numbers, so that intermediate results can have a wider range (plus or minus 2 billion).
After the calculations, we "narrow" the numbers back to 16-bit signed numbers. But
even here, the end result should ideally be within [-10000, +10000], so it isn't too loud.
</a>
    </comp>
  </circuit>
