  <circuit name="demo-wacky-technosynth">
    <a name="appearance" val="classic"/>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(106,224)" name="Callout">
      <a name="background" val="#fbfc025e"/>
      <a name="dx" val="27"/>
      <a name="dy" val="38"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#d15c5a"/>
      <a name="text">Click A or B, or both!
You can also just type the letter.</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(204,485)" name="Callout">
      <a name="background" val="#fbfc025e"/>
      <a name="dx" val="-58"/>
      <a name="dy" val="-48"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#d15c5a"/>
      <a name="text">Play with the frequency sliders.
Try keeping one of them close
to zero, this produces 
"low frequency oscillator" effect!</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(40,37)" name="Text">
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">Buttons allow you to selectively enable or pause either of two
generators: a triangle generator, or a sawtooth generator.

The results are mixed by bitwise-ORing the signals together. If one is
paused, it garbles the other. Bitwise-OR is *terrible* for mixing.

If both are playing, they mix with a strange techno beat, espcially
if one of the frequencies is close to zero.</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(546,457)" name="Callout">
      <a name="dx" val="-190"/>
      <a name="dy" val="-94"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="text">The two waves are mixed together by
"bitwise OR" which is a *terrible* way
to mix audio. But whatever!</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="1" loc="(430,300)" name="Clock">
      <a name="facing" val="north"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="1" loc="(430,380)" name="Clock">
      <a name="facing" val="north"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="2" loc="(380,350)" name="OR Gate">
      <a name="width" val="16"/>
    </comp>
