      <polyline fill="none" points="58,97 78,77" stroke="#000000"/>
      <polyline fill="none" points="98,77 98,97" stroke="#000000"/>
      <circ-port height="8" pin="160,260" width="8" x="46" y="56"/>
      <circ-port height="10" pin="420,250" width="10" x="125" y="85"/>
      <circ-port height="8" pin="160,360" width="8" x="46" y="66"/>
      <circ-anchor facing="east" height="6" width="6" x="127" y="87"/>
    </appear>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(185,130)" name="Callout">
      <a name="background" val="#feffa189"/>
      <a name="dx" val="37"/>
      <a name="dy" val="98"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="halign" val="right"/>
      <a name="text">The heart of the circuit,
an 16 bit counter configured
to start counting at 100, 
count up to 200,
then reset back to 0
and start over again.
</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(30,33)" name="Text">
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">a 80 Hz sawtooth wave generator - When enabled, counts from -10000 to +10000, then
resets to 0 and counts up again, forever. It counts by 100 per step, so at 16 kHz simulation speed,
this produces an approx 80 Hz tone (because it has 201 steps, and 16000/201 = 79.6).</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(388,158)" name="Callout">
      <a name="background" val="#feffa189"/>
      <a name="dx" val="-32"/>
      <a name="dy" val="69"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="text">This handy "linear map" is configured
to take values between [0, 200] and adjust 
them so they fall within [-10000, +1000]</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(43,424)" name="Text">
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">Q: Can we instead make the counter start at 0 and count to 100, then go to -100?
A: Sadly, Logisim "counter" components can't do that directly. They always reset to 0. 
This is what the "subtract 100" is for. (Except, see sawtooth-80hz-alternate for a slightly
different approach, which loads -100 into the counter whenever it reaches +100.)

Q: Ok, but why not at least configure the counter to count by 100 at a time?
A: Because Logisim "counter" commponents always count by 1.
This is what the "multiply by 100" is for. The trick is that "count-by-one-then-multiply-by-100"
achieves the same result as "count-by-100".

Q: Why do we count by 100? Why not count by 75? Or 150? Or anything else?
A: Good question! Try it and see what happens! </a>
