  <circuit name="triangle-62.5hz">
    <a name="appearance" val="custom"/>
    <appear>
      <rect fill="none" height="55" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2" width="80" x="50" y="55"/>
      <text dominant-baseline="central" font-family="SansSerif" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" x="78" y="104">62.5Hz</text>
      <text dominant-baseline="central" font-family="SansSerif" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="89" y="69">triangle</text>
      <polyline fill="none" points="98,98 118,78" stroke="#000000"/>
      <polyline fill="none" points="78,78 98,98" stroke="#000000"/>
      <polyline fill="none" points="58,98 78,78" stroke="#000000"/>
      <polyline fill="none" points="55,88 121,88" stroke="#b0b0b0"/>
      <visible-rgbled height="10" path="/RGBLED(210,260)" stroke-width="0" width="10" x="53" y="57"/>
      <circ-port height="8" pin="200,230" width="8" x="46" y="56"/>
      <circ-port height="10" pin="620,220" width="10" x="125" y="85"/>
      <circ-port height="8" pin="200,290" width="8" x="46" y="66"/>
      <circ-anchor facing="east" height="6" width="6" x="127" y="87"/>
    </appear>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(265,152)" name="Callout">
      <a name="background" val="#feffa189"/>
      <a name="dx" val="24"/>
      <a name="dy" val="43"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="halign" val="right"/>
      <a name="text">A counter, the
heart of the circuit.
It counts from 0 to 255,
then resets to 0 again. </a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(30,28)" name="Text">
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">a somewhat clumsy 62.5 hz triangle wave generator - When enabled, it counts up to around 10000, then counts back
down to around -10000, then back up again, forever. This counts through exactly 256 steps, counting by 80 each step.
At 16kHz simulation speed, this produces a 62.5 Hz triangle wave (since it goes through 256 steps, and 16000/512 = 62.5).</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(339,345)" name="Callout">
      <a name="dx" val="67"/>
      <a name="dy" val="-76"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="halign" val="right"/>
      <a name="text">(2) But then as we count from x=128 to x=255, we do
something different. We take (192-x), before the
multiplication. This gives the downward sequence:
192-128=64   ---&gt; times 150 ---&gt; 9600
192-129=63    --&gt; times 150 ---&gt; 9450
192-130=62   ---&gt; times 150 ---&gt; 9300
etc etc etc
192-253=-61   ---&gt; times 2 ---&gt; -9150
192-254=-62   ---&gt; times 2 ---&gt; -9300
192-255=-63   ---&gt; times 2 ---&gt; -9450</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(488,91)" name="Callout">
      <a name="dx" val="-55"/>
      <a name="dy" val="82"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">(1) The trick here is cute...
First we count from x=0 to x=127. For those values, 
we subtract 64, to get a result between -64 and 63.
After the mux, this is multiplied by 150 to get a value
in the range [-9600, +9450].</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(513,317)" name="Callout">
      <a name="dx" val="-72"/>
      <a name="dy" val="18"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">(3) Last piece... this comparison, and the MUX, 
is deciding whether to use  "x-64" or "192-x"
before the multiply-by-150. The decision is
based on a comparison:
 if x &gt; 127  then use "x-64"
 otherwise use "192-x"</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="1" loc="(200,230)" name="Pin">
      <a name="behavior" val="pullup"/>
      <a name="label" val="Enable"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="1" loc="(200,290)" name="Pin">
