  <circuit name="lowpass-filter-onepole">
    <a name="appearance" val="custom"/>
    <appear>
      <rect fill="none" height="60" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2" width="70" x="50" y="50"/>
      <text dominant-baseline="central" fill="#404040" font-family="SansSerif" font-size="10" text-anchor="start" x="70" y="102">beta</text>
      <text dominant-baseline="top" font-family="SansSerif" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="86" y="54">lowpass</text>
      <text dominant-baseline="central" font-family="SansSerif" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" x="84" y="77">filter</text>
      <circ-port height="8" pin="210,370" width="8" x="76" y="106"/>
      <circ-port height="8" pin="90,440" width="8" x="46" y="76"/>
      <circ-port height="10" pin="530,480" width="10" x="115" y="75"/>
      <circ-anchor facing="east" height="6" width="6" x="117" y="77"/>
    </appear>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(15,25)" name="Text">
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">This is a low-pass filter, with a parameter beta to control where the dropoff is.
The parameter beta is a 16-bit unsigned number, from 0 to 65535, but we
treat it mathematically as if it were a percentage from 0% (0) to 100% (65535). 
The filter equation works like this:
x[1], x[2], ..., x[n-1], x[n] are the input samples, with x[n] being the most recent.
y[1], y[2], ..., y[n-1], y[n] are the output samples, with y[n] being the most recent.
To compute the output y[n] for the current step:
       y[n] = (y[n-1]) + beta * (x[n] - y[n-1])
               = most_recent_output + beta * (how_badly_wrong_is_that_output)
In other words, the output doesn't change when the input changes, exactly. Instead,
the output now equals the previous output, plus a "little bit" depending on how far off
the previous output was from the current input. And beta controls whether we mix in a tiny
bit, a moderate amount, or a lot of the diference, to either quickly adatpt to changes, or slowly.</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(172,430)" name="Text">
      <a name="text" val="x[n]"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(239,268)" name="Callout">
      <a name="dx" val="41"/>
      <a name="dy" val="82"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="text">The alpha parameter is a 16-bit unsigned number,
from 0 to 65535. We scale it up to
be within the range [0, 2billion].</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(420,361)" name="Callout">
      <a name="dx" val="-22"/>
      <a name="dy" val="70"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#cc45bf"/>
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">This multiplication is configured as
"scale to range" meaning you can
think of one of the inputs as being
a percentage. That is, alpha is 0-100%.</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(470,468)" name="Text">
      <a name="text" val="y[n]"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(70,600)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#e2ff3d76"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#f241c4"/>
      <a name="halign" val="left"/>
      <a name="text">This circuit uses some tricks:
 * the full range for the input signal is [-32768, +32767], same for the output.
 * but internally, we use 32-bit numbers to hold these values and do arithmetic. Those
    have a much larger range, [-2billion, +2billion].
 * for beta, we scale it up too, so it falls in the range [0, +2billion]. And, when multiplying
   by beta, we use a special multiplier that is configured to "scale to range", meaning
   it treats one of the two inputs as a fraction from 0.0 to 1.0, rather than an as integer.

The reason for using 32-bit numbers is to avoid "overflow" cases. If we multiply 
two large numbers during some intermediate calculation, the intermediate result
might not fit in 16 bits, even if later we will divide or scale the result back down
to a more reasonable number. By using 32-bit values for arithmetic, we have plenty
of "headroom".  At the end of the calculations, we narrow back to 16 bits (any
values outside the 16-bit range get "clipped" to the max or min at that point, but
that probably won't happen unless the arithmetic goes haywire.)
</a>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="1" loc="(200,520)" name="Clock">
      <a name="facing" val="north"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="1" loc="(210,370)" name="Pin">
      <a name="label" val="beta"/>
      <a name="width" val="16"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="1" loc="(530,480)" name="Pin">
      <a name="facing" val="west"/>
      <a name="label" val="y"/>
      <a name="labelloc" val="east"/>
      <a name="radix" val="10unsigned"/>
      <a name="type" val="output"/>
      <a name="width" val="16"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="1" loc="(90,440)" name="Pin">
      <a name="label" val="x"/>
      <a name="radix" val="10unsigned"/>
      <a name="width" val="16"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="5" loc="(220,490)" name="Register">
      <a name="label" val="y[n-1]"/>
      <a name="width" val="32"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="8" loc="(140,440)" name="FitRange">
      <a name="normalization" val="center"/>
      <a name="typeIn" val="twosComplement"/>
      <a name="widthIn" val="16"/>
      <a name="widthOut" val="32"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="8" loc="(310,370)" name="LinearMap">
      <a name="domain" val="[0, 65535]"/>
      <a name="range" val="[0, 2147483647]"/>
      <a name="widthIn" val="16"/>
      <a name="widthOut" val="32"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="8" loc="(310,450)" name="SaturatingSubtractor">
